
// Add a comma to a number - exam question
// Max Score: 4 | Difficulty: Not Specified
// When dealing with currency, a comma is required to help group 3 digits together.

// For example, 1000 = 1,000 | 1000000 = 1,000,000

// Write a program that will take an integer as an input, calculate how many commas are needed, add them into the correct places, and output the result. A second input will contain a symbol that should be added to the front of the string, such as a $ symbol. If this input a space, add nothing to the front of the string.

// Expected input:
// An integer (1-8 digits long)
// A character
// e.g. 23456
// $
// Expected output:
// $23,456

DECLARE num:STRING
DECLARE char:CHAR
DECLARE len1:STRING
DECLARE len2:STRING
DECLARE len3:STRING
INPUT num
INPUT char
IF LENGTH(num)>6 THEN
  len3<-SUBSTRING(num,LENGTH(num)-2,3)
  len2<-SUBSTRING(num,LENGTH(num)-5,3)
  len1<-SUBSTRING(num,1,LENGTH(num)-6)
  OUTPUT char,len1,len2,len3
ELSE
  IF LENGTH(num)>3 AND LENGTH(num)<7 THEN
  len1<-SUBSTRING(num,1,LENGTH(num)-3)
  len2<-SUBSTRING(num,LENGTH(num)-2,3)
    OUTPUT char,len1,len2
  ELSE 
    OUTPUT char,num
  ENDIF
ENDIF